===Noun===
{{en-noun|corrachs}}

# {{altform|en|currach||light, rowed Irish boat}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1862|journal=University Magazine: A Literary and Philosophic Review|page=68
|passage=The '''corrachs''', as may be supposed, drew but few feet of water.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1891|author=Mary Banim|title=Here and There Through Ireland|section=part 1, page 116
|passage={{...}} the '''corrachs''' are so light, and the western fishermen are so skilful in their management, that accidents are almost unknown, and if a boatman but says it is safe to venture out with him, there is no danger whatever [...]}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1892|journal=The Eclectic Magazine of Foreign Literature, Science, and Art|volume=119|page=486
|passage=So, happy at heart, he stepped into the '''''corrach''''', that floated lightly on its own light shadow under the edge of the island, and he rowed it over the Dark Lake {{...}} but the man who sat in the '''''corrach''''' watched for the light {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1903|journal=Country Life Illustrated|volume=13|page=544
|passage=The earliest boat, so they say in Ireland, was a raft. After that came the '''corrach'''. The pattern of the '''corrach''' drifted across to the adjacent island, where in debased form it became the [[coracle]], which is used on the upper reaches of the Severn and elsewhere even unto this day. So also the '''corrach''' of ...}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1920|author=Katharine Tynan|title=Denys the Dreamer|page=247
|passage=... as the '''corrach''' pulled near the shore.}}

